    <link type="seealso" xref="printing"/>
    <desc>Use this computer's TrueType fonts on a label, get them onto the
    printer, and manage the fonts the printer holds.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Fonts on the label and on the printer</title>

  <p>Text can be printed in the printer's built-in fonts, or in any TrueType
  font installed on this computer. A TrueType font has to be on the printer
  as well, and LinuxZPL sends it there when it prints.</p>

  <section id="which">
    <title>Which fonts can be chosen</title>
    <p>Only TrueType fonts, in <file>.ttf</file> files. OpenType, Type 1 and
    font collections are left out on purpose: they cannot be sent to a Zebra
    printer, and a label using one would print in some other font.</p>
    <p><guiseq><gui>Settings</gui><gui>Local Fonts…</gui></guiseq> shows how
    LinuxZPL finds this computer's fonts. It asks fontconfig first; where
    fontconfig is missing or finds nothing, it looks through the usual font
    folders itself, and the window lists what it found there.
    <gui>Extra Font Folder…</gui> adds one folder of your own to that search,
    and <gui>Rescan</gui> looks again.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="check">
    <title>Before printing: does the printer have them?</title>
    <p>Choosing a font never waits on the printer. When you print, LinuxZPL
    asks the printer which fonts it holds and compares them with the ones the
    label uses:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title>Some are missing</title>
        <p>It lists them. <gui>Upload &amp; Print</gui> sends each one and
        then prints; <gui>Print Anyway</gui> prints without them, and the
        printer then uses a font of its own in their place.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>A font from an opened file</title>
        <p>A label file names its fonts but does not contain them. When this
        computer has no font file that matches, the font is marked
        <gui>(source file unknown)</gui> and cannot be sent.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>The printer does not answer</title>
        <p>It says so, and offers <gui>Print Anyway</gui> or
        <gui>Cancel</gui>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>On the printer, a font is named from its file name: upper case, letters,
    digits, <sys>_</sys> and <sys>-</sys> only, at most eight characters, so
    <file>DejaVuSans.ttf</file> becomes <sys>DEJAVUSA.TTF</sys>. Two fonts
    whose names would clash on one label get a number to tell them
    apart.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="memory">
    <title>Where on the printer fonts go</title>
    <p><gui>Font memory</gui>, under
    <guiseq><gui>Settings</gui><gui>Default Printer…</gui></guiseq> and in the
    Print window, chooses which of the printer's memories the fonts you pick
    are sent to, such as Flash (<sys>E:</sys>), which keeps them when the
    printer is switched off, or RAM (<sys>R:</sys>), which does not. Fonts in
    a label you opened stay where that label says they are.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="manager">
    <title>The fonts the printer holds</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>Printer</gui><gui>Fonts…</gui></guiseq> lists the
    TrueType fonts stored on the printer, in each of its memories.
    <gui>Upload…</gui> sends an installed font to the memory you choose,
    <gui>Delete</gui> removes the selected one, and <gui>Refresh</gui> asks
    again. The line at the bottom says which memories were empty and which
    could not be read, so a short list is never mistaken for a complete
    one.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="font-zero">
    <title>The printer's scalable font 0</title>
    <p>Labels made by other programs often use font 0, the printer's own
    scalable font. LinuxZPL has no copy of it, and measures it with a
    near match, Nimbus Sans Narrow Bold, when that is installed (on Debian and
    Ubuntu, the <sys>fonts-urw-base35</sys> package). Without it, font 0 text
    is drawn much wider than it prints.</p>
  </section>
</page>
